<chapter xml:id="ch-tips-on-reading-a-scientific-paper" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>* Tips on Reading a Scientific Paper</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
      <term>***This chapter is under construction.***</term>
      </p>

      <p>
        This chapter is based on the work of Madeline Moberg and Maddie Foote, two Bates students from the Class of 2019 who conceived of this topic and developed a presentation on it. Their original presentation appears <url href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1klqLyQfKPnj7DdbI2oOz_XpBFYVJ7jZL/view?usp=sharing">here.</url> They happily agreed to share this work widely.
      </p>
      <p>
        Scientific papers, also called scientific articles, come directly from active researchers. These papers typically present new research, analyze and compile existing research into a comprehensive review, or comment on trends in a given field. They tend to have a very specific structure, often base their research on data, and may present experiments and results. Because they are peer reviewed and published in academic journals, these articles are notably distinct from general online content. Scientific papers are the main sources for finding new or foundational scientific information.
      </p>
      <p>
        Once you have completed all or most of the core modeling concepts in <xref ref="part-core-modeling-concepts"/>, you may choose to develop your own projects that require datasets on outbreaks. Or you may find that you would like to keep reading scientific articles describing mathematical models of outbreaks, beyond the articles shared in this textbook. This chapter is here to help. It starts by describing ways to locate and select peer-reviewed research articles. We then discuss the typical sections in a research paper, building understanding of the goals of each of those sections. Next, we discuss possible orders in which to read scientific articles: start-to-finish is not always the most efficient or helpful for our goals. Finally, to pull everything together, we practice different information-gathering strategies by focusing on one specific research article.
      </p>
