    </introduction>

    
    <section xml:id="sec-finding-and-selecting-articles">
      <title>Finding and Selecting Articles</title>

      <p>
        As you begin investigating your own projects, it will be very important for you to seek out scientific papers. These may provide you with necessary datasets, they may show you ideas for how other people mathematically modeled outbreaks with features you have not studied before, or they may describe background or history on the subject you are building a model to study. How best can you find these kinds of papers?
      </p>
      <p>
        Most importantly, <em>use quality search tools</em>. Peer-reviewed papers are the place to find professional research, and a generic web search rarely shows these. 
      </p>
      <p>
        If you are connected with a college or university, use the search options provided to you through that campus. Colleges and universities often pay a significant amount of money to gain access to peer-reviewed research articles so that you can use them. You may need to log in to a portal using campus credentials, and you can then access search engines meant to help you find the articles you need. Check with library or information technology (IT) staff to learn how to do this on your campus.
      </p>
      <p>
        Look for articles from government health organizations, or articles published in journals related to public health, mathematical biology, or theoretical biology. Public health venues are likely to provide background, history, and data on diseases. Mathematical biology and theoretical biology journals share models used for studying disease outbreaks or endemic illnesses.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you do not have access to college or university searches, or as another way to search, seek out Open Access peer-reviewed articles. Open Access means that the articles are freely available online. One place to seek such articles is through PLOS, the Public Library of Science, at <url href="https://plos.org/" visual="plos.org">plos.org</url>.
      </p>
      
    </section>

  </chapter>